const title = "Rooms & Exits Chapter 3 Level 9 Walkthrough: Backyard";
const description = "Rooms & Exits Chapter 3 Level 9 walkthrough for Backyard: the chair-star count that takes 13 on the two star fields, the pillow order 3-1-5-6-2 read off the newspaper and entered bottom-up, plus the balance-weight, gear, grill-button, steak and fruit-wreath puzzles and the three-letter lock we could not read cleanly.";

const faq = [
  {
    q: "What is the code in Rooms & Exits Chapter 3 Level 9 Backyard?",
    a: "Two, and they are different kinds of lock. The <b>star fields on the white storage bench</b> take the answer to <b>ENTER THE NUMBER OF STARS IN THE CHAIR</b>, which is <b>13</b>. The <b>pillow lock</b> takes the sequence <b>3 · 1 · 5 · 6 · 2</b>, which is the order of the numbers printed in the newspaper, entered starting from the bottom pillow. Both were read off screen in more than one frame.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where does 13 come from?",
    a: "From a chair in the backyard. The bench front carries two star-shaped fields with a digit each, and the instruction above them reads <b>ENTER THE NUMBER OF STARS IN THE CHAIR</b>. Counting the stars on the chair gives <b>13</b>. We read the two fields showing <b>00</b> before entry at 9:46 and <b>1</b> and <b>3</b> after entry at 9:51, and we read the same pair again from a zoomed crop of that frame.",
  },
  {
    q: "How is the pillow order entered?",
    a: "From the <b>bottom</b> of the stack, not the top. The numbers in the newspaper give the pillow order as <b>3, 1, 5, 6, 2</b>, and the recording applies them bottom-up: the bottom pillow is the third one in the newspaper order, the next up is the first, then the fifth, the sixth and the second. It is the single most common mistake in this room, because reading the same sequence top-down gives a different arrangement that the lock refuses.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the balance puzzle?",
    a: "A scale with weights and a set of instructions written as ordinal pairs: <b>9TH + 5TH + 1ST</b>, then <b>8TH + 4TH + 3RD</b>, then <b>7TH + 6TH + 2ND</b>. Read literally, each line groups weights by their position in a row rather than by their size, which is the trick of the puzzle. The lines were legible in three separate frames, so the wording is reliable even though the exact weight values are not something this page prints.",
  },
  {
    q: "What do I do with the Lighter, Tongs, Steak and Candle?",
    a: "They are the barbecue chain. The <b>Lighter</b> is picked up at 0:10 and the <b>Tongs</b> at 0:17; the <b>Steak</b> arrives at 1:01, the <b>Candle</b> at 1:38 and becomes a <b>Lit candle</b> at 1:49 once the lighter is used on it. The tongs are what lets you work the grill. The grill itself has a control panel whose instruction concerns the position of each button, so the food and the fire are two halves of the same puzzle rather than separate tasks.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where are the Gears and Buttons used?",
    a: "The <b>Gears</b> go into a mechanism on the outdoor kitchen wall; the recording shows four gear slots and the counter sitting at 4/4 once they are all in. The <b>Buttons</b> are collected in two passes, at 1:09 and 1:29, and belong to a panel that wants them placed rather than pressed in a sequence. Neither hands you a number you type anywhere, so this page does not print a code for them.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the fruit wreath on the door?",
    a: "The instruction reads <b>TO ARRANGE THE FRUIT ON THE DOOR, PAY ATTENTION TO THE COLOR OF THE FRUIT</b>. A wreath of twigs on a pale blue door holds a half lemon, a kiwi slice, a strawberry, a raspberry, a small pumpkin and clusters of red berries, and the puzzle is to order them by colour rather than by fruit type. It is a drag arrangement with no digits involved.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is there a telescope in the Backyard?",
    a: "Yes, and it is a star-counting puzzle. The recording spends time at the telescope around 4:35 and the constellation pattern it reveals is what the room's star-themed locks hang on. This is also why the bench asks for the number of stars in the chair: the room repeats its star motif across two different objects so that you read one as the clue for the other.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the reward for finishing Backyard?",
    a: "We do not print one. The recording ends at 5:55 on a THANKS FOR WATCHING card with no level-complete panel, no coin total and no scene of the exit door opening. The last interactive state we can see is the pillow arrangement at 5:33, so treat the room's payout as unseen rather than as something this page is withholding.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where does Backyard sit in Chapter 3?",
    a: "Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i> and Backyard is room <b>9 of 15</b>. It follows Kids Playroom and precedes Garden. The level's own title card in this recording reads <b>Level 9</b>, and the room is the chapter's first outdoor scene, which is why it has a different palette — brick, lawn and pool water instead of painted interiors.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long does Backyard take?",
    a: "The recording runs <b>5:56</b> and covers roughly nine distinct puzzles, several of them chained. It is longer than Kids Playroom and much less linear: the balance, the gears and the grill can be worked in any order, while the pillow order depends on the newspaper and the star fields depend on finding the chair.",
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "") },
  })),
};
